To embed thousands of document chunks efficiently, use the `embedMany` function, which processes multiple texts in a single call and automatically splits large requests when needed [4,7].

To limit concurrency (how many requests run at once), chunk your input and use a concurrency control library like `p-limit`:

```ts
import { embedMany } from 'ai';
import pLimit from 'p-limit';

const limit = pLimit(5); // max 5 concurrent requests

async function embedChunksInBatches(chunks: string[], batchSize = 100) {
  const batches = Array.from(
    { length: Math.ceil(chunks.length / batchSize) },
    (_, i) => chunks.slice(i * batchSize, (i + 1) * batchSize)
  );

  const results = await Promise.all(
    batches.map(batch =>
      limit(() =>
        embedMany({
          model: 'openai/text-embedding-3-small',
          values: batch,
        })
      )
    )
  );

  return results.flatMap(r => r.embeddings);
}
```

Use the `dimensions` parameter with the desired value (e.g., 512) in both `embed` and `embedMany` calls. The SDK forwards the requested size to the provider API without truncating the returned embeddings [1][8].

```ts
import { embed } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

const { embedding } = await embed({
  model: openai.embedding('text-embedding-3-small'),
  value: 'sunny day at the beach',
  dimensions: 512,
});
```

```ts
import { embedMany } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

const { embeddings } = await embedMany({
  model: openai.embedding('text-embedding-3-small'),
  values: ['text 1', 'text 2'],
  dimensions: 512,
});
```

To compare two embeddings for similarity, use the `cosineSimilarity` function from the AI SDK. It calculates a score between -1 and 1, where values close to 1 indicate high similarity.

```ts
import { cosineSimilarity, embed } from 'ai';

const [embedding1, embedding2] = await Promise.all([
  embed({ model: 'openai/text-embedding-3-small', value: 'sunny day at the beach' }),
  embed({ model: 'openai/text-embedding-3-small', value: 'rainy afternoon in the city' }),
]);

const similarity = cosineSimilarity(embedding1.embedding, embedding2.embedding);
console.log(`cosine similarity: ${similarity}`);
```

[1][2]

To make tool calls execute one at a time (sequentially) so later calls can observe side effects from earlier ones, set `toolCallConcurrency: 1` in your `generateText` call:

```ts
import { generateText, tool } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

const result = await generateText({
  model: "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5",
  tools: {
    // your tools here
  },
  prompt: 'Update the file, then read it.',
  toolCallConcurrency: 1, // executes tool calls sequentially
});
```

[7]

To force the model to call one specific tool, use the `toolChoice` option with `{ type: 'tool', toolName: 'yourToolName' }`:

```ts
import { generateText, tool } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

const result = await generateText({
  model: "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5",
  tools: {
    weather: tool({
      description: 'Get the weather in a location',
      inputSchema: z.object({
        location: z.string().describe('The location to get the weather for'),
      }),
      execute: async ({ location }) => ({
        location,
        temperature: 72 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 21) - 10,
      }),
    }),
  },
  toolChoice: {
    type: 'tool',
    toolName: 'weather', // Force the model to call only this tool
  },
  prompt: 'What is the weather in San Francisco?',
});
```

[1][2][7]

When a tool's `execute` function throws an error, `generateText` does **not** throw that error directly. Instead, the error is captured and appears in the result as a `tool-error` part in the steps [1].

To find out which tool failed:

1. **Check the result steps** for `tool-error` parts, which include the `toolName` and `error`:
```ts
const { steps } = await generateText({
  // ...
});

const toolErrors = steps.flatMap(step =>
  step.content.filter(part => part.type === 'tool-error'),
);

toolErrors.forEach(toolError => {
  console.log('Failed tool:', toolError.toolName); // [1]
  console.log('Error:', toolError.error);
});
```

2. **Use lifecycle callbacks** to observe tool execution and errors in real time:
```ts
await generateText({
  // ...
  onToolExecutionEnd({ toolCall, toolOutput }) {
    if (toolOutput.type === 'tool-error') {
      console.log('Tool failed:', toolCall.toolName); // [3][6]
      console.log('Error:', toolOutput.error);
    }
  },
});
```

You can limit which tools the model sees in a single call using the `activeTools` option. Pass an array with the names of the tools you want to expose:

```ts
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';
import { generateText } from 'ai';

const { text } = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-6-luna'),
  tools: my50Tools, // all 50 tools
  activeTools: ['weather', 'forecast'], // only these two are visible
});
```

Alternatively, if you want the model to dynamically discover a small subset from many tools, register them with `deferLoading: true` and use `toolSearch()`:

```ts
import { toolSearch } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';
import { generateText } from 'ai';

const search = toolSearch({ maxResults: 3 }); // limit to 3 discovered tools

const { text } = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-6-luna'),
  tools: {
    ...my50Tools,
    search, // enable tool discovery
  },
  experimental_toolCallers: {
    // route discovered tools to the search caller
    ...Object.keys(my50Tools).reduce((acc, name) => ({ ...acc, [name]: ['search'] }), {}),
  },
});
```
